.TH SYNC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
sync \- flush the buffers of every drive to disk
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sync
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sync
asks Windows to write the cached data of every drive to disk.
It reads no input and writes nothing on standard output.
.PP
It visits each drive letter, from A to Z, whose type is fixed,
removable, CD-ROM, or network and whose volume information can be read,
so a drive with no disk in it is passed over.
For each, it opens the volume,
.IR \e\e.\eX: ,
for reading and writing, calls
.B FlushFileBuffers
on it, and closes it.
A drive that cannot be opened or flushed is skipped without a message.
.PP
.B sync
takes no operands, and no option but
.BR \-\-help .
Any other arguments are ignored after a single warning on standard
error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows may show a critical-error dialog for a drive with no disk in it
while
.B sync
visits the drives.
Otherwise busybox suppresses the dialog, and
.B sync
skips the drive.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Always, even when arguments were ignored or no drive could be flushed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Flush every drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sync
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a file to a USB drive, then flush:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp release.zip F:/ && sync
.EE
.RE
.PP
Arguments are ignored, with a warning:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sync \-d; echo $?
sync: ignoring all arguments
0
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unless the process is elevated, that is, running as Administrator,
Windows usually refuses to open the volume of an internal disk, such as
the system drive, for writing, though it may allow it for a USB drive.
.B sync
ignores the failure, so without elevation it may flush only some drives
or none, and it still exits with status 0.
.PP
Only drive letters are visited.
Volumes mounted in folders, and network shares without a drive letter,
are not flushed.
.PP
Unlike GNU
.BR sync ,
this build has no
.B \-d
or
.B \-f
option and no
.I file
operands, so it cannot flush individual files.
The
.B fsync
applet takes file operands, but opens them read-only, which on Windows
flushes nothing.
See
.BR fsync (1)
for ways to flush a file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR fsync (1)
